ERR1 = "Consider adding a terminate alternative to this selective accept since it has no else, delay, or terminate alternative"
ERR2 = "Confirm this task is terminated, such as with 'Terminated, before freeing it with Unchecked_Deallocation"


def ids():
    return ("ADA95_6.3.2",)


def name(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.3.2": "Published Standards/Ada 95/6.3.2 Normal Termination",
    }[id]


def tags(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.3.2": [
            "Language: Ada",
            "Standard: Ada 95",
            "Concurrency",
        ],
    }.get(id, [])


def detailed_description(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.3.2": """
<p><b>Guideline</b></p>
<p>&#8226; Do not create nonterminating tasks unintentionally.</p>
<p>&#8226; Explicitly shut down tasks that depend on library packages.</p>
<p>&#8226; Confirm that a task is terminated before freeing it with
<code>Ada.Unchecked_Deallocation</code>.</p>
<p>&#8226; Consider using a select statement with a terminate alternative
rather than an accept statement alone.</p>
<p>&#8226; Consider providing a terminate alternative for every selective
accept that does not require an else part or a delay.</p>
<p>&#8226; Do not declare or create a task within a user-defined Finalize
procedure after the environment task has finished waiting for other
tasks.</p>

<p><b>Example</b></p>
<p>This task will never terminate:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">---------------------------------------------------------------------
task body Message_Buffer is
   ...
begin  -- Message_Buffer
   loop
      select
         when Head /= Tail => -- Circular buffer not empty
            accept Retrieve (Value :    out Element) do
               ...
            end Retrieve;

      or
         when not ((Head  = Index'First and then
                    Tail  = Index'Last) or else
                   (Head /= Index'First and then
                    Tail  = Index'Pred(Head))    )
                 => -- Circular buffer not full
            accept Store (Value : in     Element);
      end select;
   end loop;
...
end Message_Buffer;
---------------------------------------------------------------------
</code></pre>

<p><b>Rationale</b></p>
<p>The implicit environment task does not terminate until all other
tasks have terminated. The environment task serves as a master for all
other tasks created as part of the execution of the partition; it
awaits termination of all such tasks in order to perform finalization
of any remaining objects of the partition. Thus, a partition will exist
until all library tasks are terminated.</p>
<p>A nonterminating task is a task whose body consists of a
nonterminating loop with no selective accept with terminate or a task
that depends on a library package. Execution of a subprogram or block
containing a task cannot complete until the task terminates. Any task
that calls a subprogram containing a nonterminating task will be
delayed indefinitely.</p>
<p>A task that depends on a library package cannot be forced to
terminate using a selective accept construct with alternative and
should be terminated explicitly during program shutdown. One way to
explicitly shut down tasks that depend on library packages is to
provide them with exit entries and have the main subprogram call the
exit entry just before it terminates.</p>
<p>The Ada Reference Manual, &sect;13.1.2 states that a bounded error
results from freeing a discriminated, unterminated task object. The
danger lies in deallocating the discriminants as a result of freeing
the task object. The effect of unterminated tasks containing bounded
errors at the end of program execution is undefined.</p>
<p>Execution of an accept statement or of a selective accept statement
without an else part, a delay, or a terminate alternative cannot
proceed if no task ever calls the entry(s) associated with that
statement. This could result in deadlock. Following the guideline to
provide a terminate alternative for every selective accept without an
else or a delay entails programming multiple termination points in the
task body. A reader can easily &ldquo;know where to look&rdquo; for the
normal termination points in a task body. The termination points are
the end of the body's sequence of statements and alternatives to select
statements.</p>
<p>When the environment task has been terminated, either normally or
abnormally, the language does not specify whether to await a task
activated during finalization of the controlled objects in a
partition. While the environment task is waiting for all other tasks
in the partition to complete, starting up a new task during
finalization results in a bounded error (Ada Reference Manual,
&sect;10.2). The exception Program_Error can be raised during creation
or activation of such a task.</p>

<p><b>Exceptions</b></p>
<p>If you are implementing a cyclic executive, you might need a
scheduling task that does not terminate. It has been said that no
real-time system should be programmed to terminate. This is extreme.
Systematic shutdown of many real-time systems is a desirable safety
feature.</p>
<p>If you are considering programming a task not to terminate, be
certain that it is not a dependent of a block or subprogram from which
the task's caller(s) will ever expect to return. Because entire
programs can be candidates for reuse (see Chapter 8), note that the
task (and whatever it depends upon) will not terminate. Also be certain
that for any other task that you do wish to terminate, its termination
does not await this task's termination. Reread and fully understand the
Ada Reference Manual, &sect;9.3 on &ldquo;Task Dependence-Termination of
Tasks.&rdquo;</p>

<p><b>Developer's Note</b></p>
<p>"Do not create nonterminating tasks unintentionally" and "consider
providing a terminate alternative for every selective accept that does
not require an else part or a delay" are automated together, as one
check: a selective accept (a <code>select</code> with at least one <code>accept</code>
alternative) that has none of an <code>else</code> part, a <code>delay</code> alternative,
or a <code>terminate</code> alternative is flagged. The Exceptions section
explicitly allows a deliberately nonterminating scheduling task, which
this check cannot distinguish from an accidental omission, so this is
accepted as a known, documented over-flag rather than a proof of a
defect.</p>
<p>"Confirm that a task is terminated before freeing it with
<code>Ada.Unchecked_Deallocation</code>" is automated for the direct case: a
procedure instantiating <code>Ada.Unchecked_Deallocation</code> whose object
parameter designates a task type is identified structurally (the
generic's actual parameters are found from the instantiated procedure's
own formal parameter and its type), and each call to it is flagged
unless a use of <code>'Terminated</code> appears earlier in the same enclosing
subprogram. This does not verify that the <code>'Terminated</code> check applies
to the same object being freed, nor does it recognize a task embedded
as a component of a record being deallocated (only a direct access-to-
task-type case); it also does not recognize any other way of confirming
termination (an explicit shutdown rendezvous, for example) besides
<code>'Terminated</code> itself. The instantiation and the calls to it commonly
live in different files (a package spec versus its body, or a client
package entirely), so once the declaring package is found, every file
that could legally call the instantiated procedure is checked: the
package's own spec and body, plus every file that names it in a with
clause &#8212; the complete set Ada visibility rules allow, found directly
from the package entity's own references, without needing to scan the
whole project.</p>
<p>The remaining three bullets are not automated. "Explicitly shut down
tasks that depend on library packages" and "do not declare or create a
task within a user-defined Finalize procedure after the environment
task has finished waiting for other tasks" both require whole-program,
runtime-lifecycle knowledge (what shuts the task down, and when during
finalization a given call occurs) that no structural check can see.
"Consider using a select statement with a terminate alternative rather
than an accept statement alone" is a design-choice recommendation with
no violation shape, distinct from the more concrete "missing terminate
on an existing selective accept" signal automated above.</p>
""",
    }[id]


def test_entity(file):
    return file.kind().check("Ada File")


def test_language(language):
    return language == "Ada"


_OPENER_KEYWORDS = ("if", "case", "loop", "select", "begin", "do")
_ALTERNATIVE_KEYWORDS = ("accept", "delay", "terminate")


def _next_significant(lex):
    lex = lex.next()

    while lex and lex.token() in ("Whitespace", "Newline", "Comment"):
        lex = lex.next()

    return lex


def _is_statement_start(lex):
    prev = lex.previous(True, True)

    return prev is None or prev.text() in (";", "then", "else", "loop", "begin", "do")


def _scan_alternative(lex):
    if lex and lex.token() == "Keyword" and lex.text().lower() == "when":
        lex = _next_significant(lex)

        while lex and lex.text().lower() not in _ALTERNATIVE_KEYWORDS:
            lex = _next_significant(lex)

    alt_kind = lex.text().lower() if lex else None

    return alt_kind, _next_significant(lex) if lex else None


def _scan_select_alternatives(lex):
    # See ADA95_6.2.2/ADA95_6.2.4/ADA95_6.2.5 for the depth-counting
    # rationale: a single counter over if/case/loop/select/begin/do,
    # closed by any "end", is enough to skip nested constructs and find
    # this select's own top-level "or"/"else"/"end select".
    alt_kind, lex = _scan_alternative(lex)
    alt_kinds = [alt_kind]
    has_else = False
    depth = 0

    while lex:
        text = lex.text().lower() if lex.token() == "Keyword" else None

        if text in _OPENER_KEYWORDS:
            depth += 1
            lex = _next_significant(lex)
            continue

        if text == "end":
            if depth == 0:
                return alt_kinds, has_else

            depth -= 1
            lex = _next_significant(lex)

            # "end if"/"end case"/"end loop"/"end select" must not have
            # their own completer keyword mistaken for a fresh opener.
            if (
                lex
                and lex.token() == "Keyword"
                and lex.text().lower() in ("if", "case", "loop", "select")
            ):
                lex = _next_significant(lex)

            continue

        if depth == 0 and text == "or":
            lex = _next_significant(lex)
            alt_kind, lex = _scan_alternative(lex)
            alt_kinds.append(alt_kind)
            continue

        if depth == 0 and text == "else":
            has_else = True

        lex = _next_significant(lex)

    return alt_kinds, has_else


def _find_selects_missing_terminate(lexer, start_line, end_line):
    results = []
    lex = lexer.lexeme(start_line, 1)

    while lex and lex.line_begin() <= end_line:
        if (
            lex.token() == "Keyword"
            and lex.text().lower() == "select"
            and _is_statement_start(lex)
        ):
            select_line, select_col = lex.line_begin(), lex.column_begin()
            alt_kinds, has_else = _scan_select_alternatives(_next_significant(lex))

            is_selective_accept = "accept" in alt_kinds
            has_terminate_or_delay = "terminate" in alt_kinds or "delay" in alt_kinds

            if is_selective_accept and not has_else and not has_terminate_or_delay:
                results.append((select_line, select_col))

        lex = lex.next()

    return results


def _task_ranges(file):
    ranges = []

    for decl_ref in file.filerefs("Declare Body", "Task"):
        ent = decl_ref.ent()
        end_ref = ent.ref("Endby Body")

        if not end_ref:
            continue

        ranges.append((decl_ref.line(), end_ref.line(), ent))

    return ranges


def _check_missing_terminate(check, file):
    lexer = file.lexer(lookup_ents=False)

    for start, end, task_ent in _task_ranges(file):
        for select_line, select_col in _find_selects_missing_terminate(
            lexer, start, end
        ):
            check.violation(task_ent, file, select_line, select_col, ERR1)


def _instantiates_unchecked_deallocation(ent):
    for generic_ref in ent.refs("Instanceof"):
        if str(generic_ref.ent().longname()) == "Ada.Unchecked_Deallocation":
            return True

    return False


def _frees_a_task(proc_ent):
    for param_ref in proc_ent.refs("Define, Declare", "Parameter"):
        param_ent = param_ref.ent()

        for typed_ref in param_ent.refs("Typed"):
            access_ent = typed_ref.ent()

            for designated_ref in access_ent.refs("Typed"):
                if designated_ref.ent().kind().check("Task"):
                    return True

    return False


def _candidate_caller_files(proc_ent):
    # Any file that can call this procedure must have visibility to the
    # package that declares it: either it is that package's own spec or
    # body, or it names the package in a with clause. Ada requires a
    # direct "with" for direct name visibility, so this is the complete
    # set of files that could possibly call it, not a heuristic subset -
    # and both ref kinds are available straight from the package entity,
    # without needing to scan every file in the project.
    pkg_ent = proc_ent.parent()
    files = {}

    for ref in pkg_ent.refs("Declare Spec, Declare Body, Withby"):
        files[ref.file().id()] = ref.file()

    return files.values()


def _subprogram_ranges(file):
    ranges = []

    for decl_ref in file.filerefs(
        "Declare Body", "Procedure, Function, Task, Entry"
    ):
        ent = decl_ref.ent()
        end_refs = [r for r in ent.refs("End") if r.file().id() == file.id()]

        if not end_refs:
            continue

        ranges.append((decl_ref.line(), max(r.line() for r in end_refs)))

    return ranges


def _innermost_range(line, ranges):
    home = None

    for start, end in ranges:
        if start <= line <= end:
            if home is None or (end - start) < (home[1] - home[0]):
                home = (start, end)

    return home


def _has_terminated_check_before(lexer, start_line, before_line):
    lex = lexer.lexeme(start_line, 1)

    while lex and lex.line_begin() < before_line:
        if lex.text() == "'":
            next_lex = _next_significant(lex)

            if next_lex and next_lex.text().lower() == "terminated":
                return True

        lex = lex.next()

    return False


def _check_calls_to(check, proc_ent):
    for call_file in _candidate_caller_files(proc_ent):
        lexer = call_file.lexer(lookup_ents=False)
        subprogram_ranges = _subprogram_ranges(call_file)

        for call_ref in call_file.filerefs("Call", "Procedure"):
            if call_ref.ent().id() != proc_ent.id():
                continue

            enclosing = _innermost_range(call_ref.line(), subprogram_ranges)
            start = enclosing[0] if enclosing else call_ref.line()

            if not _has_terminated_check_before(lexer, start, call_ref.line()):
                check.violation(
                    None, call_file, call_ref.line(), call_ref.column(), ERR2
                )


def _check_unconfirmed_free(check, file):
    for decl_ref in file.filerefs("Declare", "Procedure"):
        ent = decl_ref.ent()

        if _instantiates_unchecked_deallocation(ent) and _frees_a_task(ent):
            _check_calls_to(check, ent)


def check(check, file):
    _check_missing_terminate(check, file)
    _check_unconfirmed_free(check, file)
